"Costs and risks": each with the item it comes from. Examples:
- Every item costs time on every call; if the check runs on 100 calls and catches nothing, it is pure tax. Mitigation: one filesystem pass, no subprocess/network; item 8 reads the canary log rather than re-running it (my #8, my #14).
- A false failure blocks a good brief and costs a round; a shape check that is stricter than the grader will reject briefs the grader would accept (my #16 — enforce only what is written; my #10 — don't strike the solid).
- Checking paths/anchors can be gamed by a brief that names the right files but doesn't use them; the check reduces, does not remove, the risk (my #2 — you can only make yourself unbeatable, not make the enemy certain to be beaten).
- Item 4 (contiguous numbering) can fail a list that is correct but intentionally numbered with gaps; then the check is wrong, not the list. Need EJ's agreement on the invariant (my #13 — not every road is taken; a check that fits one list is not universal).
- Item 7 (withholding) requires the deny-list to be written; a missing or stale deny-list gives false confidence (my #11 — foreknowledge must come from the actual source).
- The biggest risk: the check becomes a second copy of the grader's constants and the two drift apart; fixing a heading in one place breaks the other silently. The mitigation is one shared source (my #16 — orders clear and enforced from one copy; my #12 — fix the structure).
- Pre-flight can create a false sense of safety and encourage the main agent to write sloppier briefs (moral hazard) (my #2 — the unbeatable half is yours; the check is not the whole victory).
- If the check fixes nothing (it never edits), every failure costs a manual repair; that is deliberate (the brief says it never fixes the file) (my #13 — the general does not accept every command; the check reports, the main agent decides).

Now let me write the "How I decided" as numbered steps, each citing my #n or "my reading". I want maybe 10-12 steps that both explain the method and reference the specific facts (files) I read. Let me draft:

1. I took the check as a reckoning before the call: count what the brief carries (files, anchors, items, headings) before committing an engine (my #1). The fact that the check has no AI call and can only pass/fail sets its kind: it can measure the artifact's ground, not its meaning (my reading).
2. I asked what would make the call unwinnable no matter how good the engine is — a missing file, a stale contract, a write the engine cannot make — and put those first, because the check can own them and the engine cannot (my #2).
3. I read the two artifacts (`assets/brief.md`, `brief-plain.md`, `docs/workflow-templates/node-brief.md`) and the runner (`six_experts.py`) and looked for the places where the brief and the grader are two copies of the same fact: the headings `SECTIONS` vs "Write exactly this"; the item count in the brief vs `n_items`' `max`; the result path vs the engine's write mode. Those gaps are the empty ground the pre-flight should strike (my #10, my reading of the files).
4. I kept only items where a script can tell yes/no with filesystem/text work, because the check must be cheap on every call (my #8, my #14). A check that needs a subprocess or the network is readiness, not pre-flight.
5. I placed the item-order check (my #1's counting, my #14's proportion) because the post-check's whole citation guard rests on `n_items = max`, so a gap in the list quietly disarms it (my reading of `six_experts.py:77-79`, `check_one`).
6. I used the engine table for the capability items (`EXECUTOR_KINDS.md`): read-only/plan forms cannot write; opencode cannot read outside the project; qwen/agy can exit 0 with no file. The brief must match the force (my #4).
7. I made the check's output a per-item failure, not a single bit, so the main agent can repair the named thing and the check can be sabotage-proven per item (my #17; the house sabotage rule).
8. I left judgment out deliberately: one-task, truth, quality, voice, whether the engine is up. A gate that asks for these is the walled city, the method for when there is no alternative (my #3), and it would demand of the brief what only the run can show (my #11, my #12).
9. I required the check to be able to fail, proven by sabotage and by replay over the existing run corpus, because a check that has only ever passed is not known to be a check (my #17; EXECUTOR_KINDS canary sabotage).
10. I judged the result by the unit the project already uses — engine calls and caught failures — not by the size of the checklist (my #20).
11. I would make this a change to the pattern: today the brief-writing piece has no check of its own; the pre-flight is its check, and its result belongs in the state file (my #16 — orders clear and enforced; *proposed change to the pattern*).

